%	SECTION 2
%----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\section{Previous work on DA for Wave Forecasts}
\label{sec:prevwork}

Several studies have been completed previously investigating the effectiveness of DA methods to improve wave forecasts. In this section, some of these studies and their findings are summarised, and the differences between this study and pervious ones are discussed.

\cite{Caires2018} investigated the effect on wave forecasts of using an ensemble Kalman filter (EnKF) method to adjust the wind fields used as forcing in a wave model. They used hourly observations from six locations. Their results are shown in figure\ref{fig:caires2018fig30}.

An improvement is seen in forecasts of wave height and period, but it is relatively small compared to other studies and short lived, not extending past 24hrs in any instance (check). This may be due to the indirect relationship between the assimilated wind data and the forecasted wave variables. We hope that assimilating the forecasted variables directly will lead to improved performance over this wind-based approach.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width = \textwidth]{Figures/caires2018fig30}
\caption{RMSE in wave height (left) and period (right) from two different observation locations. Figure 30 from \cite{Caires2018}.}
\label{fig:caires2018fig30}
\end{figure}

Another possible reason for the relatively poor performance in this case is the small number of observations used. Observations were available hourly from only six different locations, while operational DA systems commonly assimilate upwards of $10^6$ observations at a time. This study is also limited in this regard, as data is only available from on buoy. A study which used many more observations is \cite{Houghton2022}, in which an optimal interpolation (OI) method (cite) was used to compare the effects of assimilating wave height and spectra. In this study, observations came from the global Sofar spotter buoy network, which contains over 600 buoys. A plot of their results is shown in figure \ref{fig:houghton2022fig3}.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width = \textwidth]{Figures/houghton2022fig3}
\caption{RMSE from spectral DA and wave height ($H_s$) DA, normalised by the RMSE without DA. Results shown for significant wave height $H_s$, peak period $T_p$, mean period $T_m$, directional spread $\sigma_\theta$, peak direction $D_p$ and mean direction $D_m$. Figure 3 from \citet{Houghton2022}.}
\label{fig:houghton2022fig3}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:houghton2022fig3} shows that both variables give very similar improvements in predictions of wave height, while spectral assimilation gives better results for other quantities such as period and direction. They also found that the spectral DA did particularly well in swell-dominated fields when compared to assimilating wave height. This shows that the type of observations assimilated can have a large effect on the performance of a DA system.

In this project we assimilate two measures of period, $T_{m01}$ and $T_{m02}$. These variables are related to different moments of the spectrum (see equations \ref{eqn:moments} - \ref{eqn:mainvars}), so we hope that assimilating both $T_{m01}$ and $T_{m02}$ as well as wave height will have some of the benefits of spectral DA identified in \cite{Houghton2022}. Since assimilating a full wave spectrum involves much more data than bulk variables such as wave height and period, we hope also that our method will incur less computational cost than assimilating a full spectrum.

To investigate the effect of the DA method used, \cite{Houghton2023} assimilated wave height observations from satellite altimeters and Sofar spotter buoys. They compared two different DA methods, OI and the ensemble-based local ensemble transform Kalman filter (LETKF) \citep{Hunt2007}. Plots of their results are included in figure \ref{fig:houghton2023fig6}.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width = .8\textwidth]{Figures/houghton2023fig6}
\caption{Forecast bias and RMSE from altimeter observations (top) and buoy observations (bottom). Figure 6 from \cite{Houghton2023}.}
\label{fig:houghton2023fig6}
\end{figure}

They found that both methods reduced forecast error and bias out to 2.5 days leadtime, with LETKF having slightly greater forecast skill overall. In addition to this, they found that the LETKF gave more physically realistic increments due to its flow dependent background error covariance matrix. A plot showing increments from each method is shown in figure \ref{fig:houghton2022fig3}. This shows that the as well as the observations, the DA method used affects DA performance. In this project we use the ensemble transform Kalman filter (ETKF) method, which is similar to the LETKF except it does not use localisation, which allows observations to impact the state over long distances. This method was chosen over LETKF partly because it leads to a simpler DA implementation, and partly because some long-distance covariances were found in the forecast data which appear to be genuine (section \ref{sec:bgerrors}).

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width = .8\textwidth]{figures/houghton2023fig3}
\caption{An example $H_s$ increment from both LETKF and OI methods. Colours show value of increment, markers show the locations of observation buoys. Figure 3 from \cite{Houghton2023}.}
\label{fig:houghton2023fig3}
\end{figure}

In this project, we apply DA to a specific forecasting context and client in the Gulf of Mexico, and so we are constrained by the observation data available in this context. As seen above, the studies with more observations saw better DA performance, and our results should be evaluated with this limitation in mind. Another limitation which none of the previous studies were subject to is that we are unable to run the wave model ourselves, which is a central step of most DA systems. We hope that the knowledge gained from these previous studies will allow us to get good results in spite of these limitations.

