To this end, we perform a twin experiment using the ensemble transform Kalman filter (ETKF) to assimilate observations of the three variables. A twin experiment is a DA experiment in which fictional obersvations are gerenated and assimilated in place of real observations (see section \ref{sec:twinexp} for details). We perform one experiment in which all three aforementioned variables are assimilated, and one experiment in which wave height alone is assimilated. The reasoning for this is that wave height extremes are of particular interest to Fugro's client due to their impact on operational safety, so we consider $H_s$ to be the most important variable of the three. We examine the results of these two experments in comparison with each other, and with the forecast data before DA, to evaluate the performance of DA in both instances.

Various aspects of the results will be examined to gain a full understanding of the DA performance. These are:

\begin{itemize}
\item Increments. The changes made to the forecast by the DA system are called increments, and they can aid understanding of how DA is modifying the model state. If the DA system is performing well, we expect to see physically realistic increments.
\item The root mean square error (RMSE) of the ensemble mean. We expect the DA to move the ensemble mean closer to the true state so RMSE will be used to quantify and test this. The dependence of RMSE on forecast leadtime and location within the domain will be examined.
\item \comment{The correlation between ensemble mean and the true state. DA should make the ensemble mean more closely correlated with the true state, so we will test this hypothesis. Correlations in space and in time will both be presented.}{Are you still making this comparison?}
\item Errors in maximum Hs. Extremes of Hs are of particular interest in these forecasts as these are the events which cause danger and \replace{require the pausing}{require the cession} of offshore operations. We will examine the effectiveness of DA to reduce error in the timings and magnitudes of wave height maxima.
\end{itemize}
\add{Furthermore, we will evaluate whether the ensemble spread provides an appropriate measure for the error in the ensemble mean.} 

\comment{}{Add a summary of what you're going to cover in the next chapters. E.g. In order to understand our DA system and its performance, we will look at ... in chapter 2. In chapter 3 the ...}

\remove{Since accuracy in forecasting Hs is safety critical for offshore operations, we consider it to be the most important variable of the three and so a separate DA run is performed where only Hs is assimilated. Results from this run are compared to results from assimilating the three variables Hs, Tm01 and Tm02 together.}
\begin{itemize}
\item The correlation between ensemble mean and the true state. DA should make the ensemble mean more closely correlated with the true state, so we will test this hypothesis.
\item Ensemble spread. This is an important measure of uncertainty in ensemble forecasts, which can give a good estimate of forecast error and allow a forecast to inform decisions with greater or lesser confidence. We hope that DA will reduce the ensemble spread, thus allowing decisions to be made with greater confidence based on the forecasts. The ensemble spread is compared to the RMSE to check for `overconfidence' in the post-DA ensemble (see section \ref{sec:DApractice}).
\item Errors in maximum $H_s$. Extremes of $H_s$ are of particular interest in these forecasts as these are the events which cause danger and require the pausing of offshore operations. We will examine the effectiveness of DA to reduce error in the timings and magnitudes of wave height maxima.
\end{itemize}
